From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ysfaculty <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 22:18:37 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 177/194] hjviewer: the PC relays only through the relay it
 offered, with a bounded relay uuid

The PC dialed whatever relay address and uuid a forwarded RequestRelay
named. hbbs forwards a controller's RequestRelay to the PC as it is, so
anyone who could get one forwarded chose the address the PC's
SYSTEM-started process connected to, and in a WebSocket build check_ws
passes a ws:// or wss:// value through unchanged: the request picked the
whole URL. The PC now accepts a RequestRelay only for a relay it put in
its own punch reply in the last ten minutes or the relay it would pick
by itself; anything else is dropped (throttled log line) before any
lookup or connect. A pinned build's relay is always the server it is
pinned to (relay-server is an overwrite setting), and a genuine
controller only echoes the relay the PC sent: the PunchHoleSent or
LocalAddr relay that hbbs copies into PunchHoleResponse, or the PC's
own RelayResponse when it starts the relay itself (WebSocket, forced
relay, the WebRTC race). Our hbbs runs without --mask, so it never
rewrites that relay for a LAN. Every genuine request therefore names
the pinned server and passes the second branch, also after the ten
minutes, so an idle PC's reconnects are not affected. The relay uuid
must be 1-128 ASCII letters, digits, '-' or '_' on both sides (the PC
for RequestRelay, the controller for RelayResponse); all our uuids are
Uuid::new_v4 strings.

Backported from rustdesk/rustdesk 2c085f91db7909cf0fa7245609460c3ca37a9306 (Only relay to addresses this peer offered, and bound relay uuids (#16858))
Against upstream's diff: the check_relay_uuid line in Client::create_relay
and the two test-module hunks placed by hand (context differs in our
series; the rendezvous_mediator tests import Duration and Instant
there). One test added: a ws:// or wss:// relay value is refused even
when its host is a relay this PC offered.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
